Our contributions are both methodological and empirical:
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{CSD}, a black-box diagnostic methodology for detecting surface-level stylistic biases amenable to content-preserving rewriting, via NLI-gated content control, per-axis style decomposition, and position-controlled pairwise comparison (\S\ref{sec:method}).
    \item \textbf{Measurement-mode divergence with per-axis confound structure:} Pairwise comparison and Likert scoring produce systematically divergent verdicts on the same style contrasts---complete divergence at moderate bias ($d \to 0$ at $n = 355$), ${>}60\%$ signal loss at stronger effects---confirmed by seven statistical methods across four judges and three benchmarks (Tables~\ref{tab:main_results}--\ref{tab:sensitivity}). GEE conditional decomposition reveals qualitatively distinct confound structures per axis: verbosity is style-dominated (Style OR $8$--$13$, Orig OR ${<}1$), formality is a conditional effect, register is judge-dependent---determining which pairwise estimates are directly interpretable and which require decomposition (Table~\ref{tab:gee}).
    \item \textbf{Practical implication:} Single-format audits risk systematic blind spots; per-axis decomposition distinguishes style-dominated from quality-confounded effects, informing which pairwise-based alignment signals require correction.
\end{itemize}

