Our contributions are both methodological and empirical:
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{CSD}, a black-box diagnostic methodology for detecting surface-level style sensitivity via NLI-gated content control, per-axis style decomposition, and position-controlled pairwise comparison (\S\ref{sec:method}).
    \item \textbf{GEE conditional decomposition} as a diagnostic layer that separates style-specific from quality-correlated components of aggregate pairwise preference, revealing qualitatively distinct confound structures per axis: verbosity is style-dominated (Style OR $8$--$13$, Orig OR ${<}1$), formality is a conditional effect, register is judge-dependent (Table~\ref{tab:gee}).
    \item \textbf{Measurement-mode divergence:} Pairwise comparison and Likert scoring produce systematically divergent verdicts---complete divergence at moderate bias ($d \to 0$ at $n = 355$), ${>}60\%$ signal loss at stronger effects---confirmed across four judges and three benchmarks (Table~\ref{tab:main_results}). Single-format audits risk systematic blind spots.
\end{itemize}

